{
  "id": "Compound::DB00973 - Disease::DOID:3393",
  "verbalization": "Ezetimibe, an antilipidemic agent, is implicated in the treatment of Atherosclerosis, a condition that ultimately leads to Coronary Artery Disease (CAD). The evidence suggests that Ezetimibe exerts its therapeutic effect by reducing low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol levels, which is a key mechanism in the development of Atherosclerosis. This reduction in cholesterol is further amplified by the use of Simvastatin or Rosuvastatin, both of which are antilipidemic agents, in the treatment of CAD. The shared ancestry of Ezetimibe and these drugs, as well as their common classification as antilipidemic agents, underscores a broader pharmacological relationship that involves lipid metabolism and cardiovascular health.\n\nThe integration of these pathways highlights a recurring mechanism: the modulation of lipid metabolism through antilipidemic agents, which in turn influences the progression of Atherosclerosis and its downstream effects on CAD. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the same end points—such as Atherosclerosis and CAD—suggests a robust and well-established therapeutic relationship between Ezetimibe and CAD. While the exact molecular mechanisms may vary between the different agents, the shared pharmacological profile and the established clinical outcomes support a consistent and significant role for Ezetimibe in the management of CAD.",
